comment.narrative:
11: Number of functional Police Posts rehabilitated, equipped and staffed with competent personnel with a service-oriented culture
12: Number of current and new Police Officers trained and exercising effective command and control disaggregated by gender and expressing satisfaction on the new skills learnt
13: Number of prosecutor offices established and functioning, and officers trained and discharging their duties impartially disaggregated by gender and age
16: % of CBRMs stating increase in interactions and communication with other peacebuilding, rule of law and justice institutions at community, locality and state levels;
17: % of community members stating increase in access to CBRMs for resolution of conflicts;
21: Numbers of institutions whose capacities on human rights-based approaches and programming strengthened

One Police post was established in Kilekile village, and a Prosecutor Office was established in Assalaya town and are functional with staff deployed 
79 Police participants (12 Female & 67 Male inclusive of 50 newly recruited in the last 12 months) had their capacities built on child, womens rights and command and control (community-based policing, public safety and security committees and police volunteer schemes, investigation / forensic capacities, case management system, gender equality, diversity, and gender-responsive policing).
One prosecutor offices established and functioning, and Prosecutors, police investigators, and judges (30 participants - 25 men; 5 women) were trained on improving coordination between prosecutors, judges, investigators, in the investigation and prosecution of sexual and gender-based violence in compliance with international standards- collection, presentation of forensic, medical, and ballistic evidence
100% of CBRMs stated an increase in interactions and communication with other peacebuilding, rule of law and justice institutions at community, locality and state levels;
100% of community members stated increase in access to CBRMs for resolution of conflicts;
A total of 10 civil society organizations (CSOs) with 43 participants (13 female, 30 male) trained on how to apply a rights-based approach to policy advocacy and programming.
